      {
        "id": 49140,
        "name": "FeelNFine",
        "username": "FeelNFine",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/feelnfine/{size}/2713_2.png",
        "created_at": "2012-12-11T03:39:18.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"RanisTheSlayer\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/r/2bfe46/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> RanisTheSlayer:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>From my perspective, I think this is only a hurdle toward spreading the word to what a great game this is.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Are you saying that you wouldn't reccomend this game to people solely because of the drawing mechanic, or because it's enough to make people not want to play again? &nbsp;I can't speak for everyone, but I had trouble with end of turn drawing because of Magic. &nbsp;I remembered not to draw at the start of my turn, but I kept forgetting to draw at the end. &nbsp;That being said, I had so much fun playing Sentinels at the shop, as did everyone else, that we immediatly played again, and by the second game it wasn't a problem. &nbsp;I think the design and feel of the game is more than enough to pull people over a little awkwardness.<br><br>Would I want other games to change it? No, half of the fun of Magic is that anticipation of your next turn, hoping to draw exactly what you need and reacting based on that. You are inherntly familiar with your deck, so you have multipul stratigies forming in your head based on what your opponet does for the potential draws you have. SotM fun doesn't come from that anticipation, but for finding your place in a heroic team and solving the problem in front of you. Like an evolving puzzle with explosions. &nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T02:22:32.552Z",
